    "prompt": "Below is a description of the Python function `LaTeXTranslator.visit_rubric`.\n\nDescription:\n\"\"\"\nThe `visit_rubric` function in the `LaTeXTranslator` class is responsible for handling the rendering of a `rubric` node in LaTeX output. A rubric is a heading-like element used in reStructuredText documents to introduce a section or topic. This function checks if the rubric contains only one node and if its text is \"Footnotes\" or its translated equivalent. If so, it raises a `SkipNode` exception to skip processing this rubric. Otherwise, it determines the appropriate LaTeX sectioning command based on the rubric's heading level, defaulting to 'subsubsection'. If the rubric has a custom 'heading-level' attribute, it attempts to map this to a corresponding LaTeX sectioning command. If the level is unsupported, it logs a warning. The function then appends the opening LaTeX command for the section to the `body` list and sets up context for closing the section. It also sets a flag `in_title` to 1, indicating that the next content will be part of a title. This function relies on the `sectionnames` list and the `top_sectionlevel` attribute to determine the correct LaTeX sectioning command. It handles unsupported heading levels by logging a warning and uses exceptions to skip processing of specific rubrics. The side effects include modifying the `body` list and setting the `in_title` flag, which affect the subsequent rendering of the document.\n\"\"\"\n\nSplit the description into atomic claims. Each claim must be a single, self-contained factual statement about the function that could be checked against the function's source code (name the function explicitly; no pronouns referring to other claims). Do not add claims that are not in the description; do not merge two facts into one claim. Keep the original wording where possible. Assign each claim exactly one type:\n- \"structural\": the function's signature, parameter names/defaults/types, return type, or 